ng these buildings look nice and  summer but then when it's winter and all  of the foliage is losing their leaves it  looks kind of mediocre and then you'd  rather wish that all of the attention  went to designing the building itself  that's wildly off-topic but I can kind  of relate it back to planet coaster  because one thing that I always feel is  kind of surprising about planet coaster  is that nobody does any winter coasters  maybe if you people have done this but  I've rarely seen it and sure people make  coasters where it's in a snowy biome but  I mean winter as in when it's winter  when a park is running in winter and  there's not necessarily any snow but all  the trees are dead and lifeless and  everything just looks generally very  different there's barely any grass and  the grass that is there it looks a  little bit more colorless overall  there's very few colors I think it's a  style which a lot of people don't do  because it obviously looks really drab  but with a lot of theme parks in the  world being open all year round yet in  winter having very colorless foliage and  very different look altogether I think a  winter park in general could be very  interesting and planet coaster and again  I'm not talking about winter in terms of  snow I think plenty of people are trying  to snow a biome but really just winter  in terms of how most places that are not  an anoxic climates or a tropical climate  look like in winter when there's not any  snow but yeah that is still a little bit  of an off topic points it's soo  something and something that I think  about sometimes because you know this is  planet coaster we can basically present  the perfect situation but all planet  coaster parks almost all of them are  always perfectly vibrant and saturated  and all of the trees have all of their  leaves and everything it's always sunny  and beautiful weather but most of the  times theme parks don't actually look  like this this is a sort of very  idealistic image of what a theme park  looks like him that that is not really  throwing any shade to anybody really  just an idea I would love to sometime  see some real winter parks or some kind  of drab looking parks where it just  gives you a cozy winter atmosphere I  think there's some charm to that as well  being in a park where the weather is  kind of bad and all the trees are kind  of dead but it's quiet and you can drink  hot chocolate sometimes that's really  awesome as well it's about time by the  way that I start getting on topic again  I don't think I've quite pointed this  out yet this is the second time that I'm  recording the commentary so I think I  only pointed it out in the Pew in the  previous time but the back of the  building here is in the sort of standard  storage shed style normally if you have  a transfer tract this is just usually  what you'd see just dressing up the  skeleton with some very simple and cheap  walls and no detailing at all and that  is what I'm doing at the back of this  building just because there will be  foliage around here plus there are no  sight lines here there's no path  anywhere near this and you're not gonna  be able to see this so I don't really  see the point's interesting this up all  too much just keeping the back of the  maintenance shed here as simple as I can  just to make sure that realistically I  wouldn't have to spend too much money on  that  it's also a little bit lower than the  rest of the building so it certainly  doesn't draw any attention toward it and  the real focus of the building is really  toward the front of it now something  which I totally forgot to talk about  which I just did a little while ago is  do the other part of the transfer tract  because there is one thing which I  didn't need to build to make sure that  you can get the cosa train either on the  track itself or onto the transfer track  is that little switch track right in  front of the station usually you see  those switch tracks behind the station  or at the end of the ride if you will  but in this case this is really the only  place where I could fit it but that  switch track is really just the more  standard type of switch track and what  you typically see on mine train coasters  as well where there's just one small  straight piece of track and one curved  one and you switch between these two to  lead the coaster train down to different  paths  usually it switches horizontally like  this sometimes they actually have a  rotating one where both tracks are on  the bottom of each other and it's kind  of like flipping a coin but yeah there's  a bunch of variations on this but this  is just the one that fits best in here  and the only one that I honestly have to  space the build and the rest of this is  just kind of finishing the last little  details on the shed adding some foliage  around it and also finishing that little  lake that we've got going in the middle  of this whole area which i think is  shaping up to be a pretty good part for  some sight lines as well  I'm trying to keep the sightlines in  mind as much as possible throughout the  building of this whole ride and one of  my favorite sight lines is honestly from  the catwalks of that little lake you  only see it for a fraction of a second  if you're on the ride itself because  even though it curves around that sight  line you'd obviously have to tilt your  head quite a bit to see the view but I  still think it's a really cool view and  something which would look really great  if you do manage to look that way if  you're on the coaster because it it is  not one of you where all the bridges  line up where you can see the station as  well as the church in the distance and  hence why I decided to have a lake there  just to make sure that that sight line  is intact because if there were just  regular foliage and trees over there you  would definitely not be able to have  that sight line so yeah sometimes I go  for a a feature in the landscape just to  make sure that you can see something  else from some other place which I think  works out in some cases in any case for  Jaeger horn the sight lines are some of  the most important parts of the park now  finally I wanted to round up this  episode by finishing this wall of  foliage on the side of the track here  just to make sure that you don't see the  edge of the park and you don't see that  wooden fence that separates the park  from the staff area and that is  basically it for this episode and that  is the transfer tract and its  surroundings finished I'd like to thank  you guys for watching and I'll see you  next time  [Music]
 

